# React Component Development Rules

This project uses React 19.1.0 with React Compiler (babel-plugin-react-compiler) integrated. Follow these rules when creating or modifying React components.

## Core Rules of React

### 1. Components Must Be Pure
- Components must be idempotent: same inputs always produce same output
- Never mutate props or state during rendering
- Side effects (API calls, subscriptions, DOM manipulation) must be in useEffect, event handlers, or other lifecycle hooks
- Never mutate variables outside the component's scope during rendering

### 2. Rules of Hooks
- **Only call hooks at the top level** - never inside loops, conditions, or nested functions
- **Only call hooks from React functions** - React components or custom hooks
- **Call hooks in the same order every render** - maintain consistent hook order
- Use ESLint plugin `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` to catch violations

### 3. Component Structure
- Use functional components with TypeScript
- Define props interfaces with clear, descriptive names (e.g., `SidebarProps`, `DashboardProps`)
- Export interfaces/types alongside components when they might be reused
- Use default exports for main component, named exports for utilities/types

### 4. Component Composition
- **Prefer composition over configuration** - build complex UIs from smaller, reusable components
- **Use children prop** for flexible component composition
- **Compose components rather than creating monolithic components**
- **Extract reusable logic into custom hooks** - share stateful logic between components
- **Use compound components pattern** when components work together as a unit

#### Composition Patterns

**1. Children Composition (Slot Pattern)**
```typescript
// Good: Flexible component with children
interface CardProps {
  title: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
  footer?: React.ReactNode;
}

const Card = ({ title, children, footer }: CardProps) => {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
      {footer && <div className="card-footer">{footer}</div>}
    </div>
  );
};

// Usage
<Card title="Dashboard" footer={<Button>Save</Button>}>
  <Chart data={data} />
</Card>
```

**2. Compound Components**
```typescript
// Good: Related components that work together
interface TabsProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  defaultTab?: string;
}

interface TabsContextType {
  activeTab: string;
  setActiveTab: (tab: string) => void;
}

const TabsContext = createContext<TabsContextType | null>(null);

const Tabs = ({ children, defaultTab = "" }: TabsProps) => {
  const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState(defaultTab);
  return (
    <TabsContext.Provider value={{ activeTab, setActiveTab }}>
      <div className="tabs">{children}</div>
    </TabsContext.Provider>
  );
};

const TabsList = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => (
  <div className="tabs-list">{children}</div>
);

const TabsTrigger = ({ value, children }: { value: string; children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  const context = useContext(TabsContext);
  if (!context) throw new Error("TabsTrigger must be inside Tabs");
  return (
    <button onClick={() => context.setActiveTab(value)}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};

// Usage
<Tabs defaultTab="tab1">
  <TabsList>
    <TabsTrigger value="tab1">Tab 1</TabsTrigger>
    <TabsTrigger value="tab2">Tab 2</TabsTrigger>
  </TabsList>
</Tabs>
```

**3. Render Props Pattern**
```typescript
// Good: Share logic through render props
interface DataFetcherProps<T> {
  url: string;
  children: (data: T | null, loading: boolean, error: Error | null) => React.ReactNode;
}

const DataFetcher = <T,>({ url, children }: DataFetcherProps<T>) => {
  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    // fetch logic
  }, [url]);
  
  return <>{children(data, loading, error)}</>;
};

// Usage
<DataFetcher url="/api/users">
  {(data, loading, error) => {
    if (loading) return <Loader />;
    if (error) return <Error message={error.message} />;
    return <UserList users={data} />;
  }}
</DataFetcher>
```

**4. Custom Hooks for Logic Composition**
```typescript
// Good: Extract reusable logic
const useDataFetching = <T,>(url: string) => {
  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    // fetch logic
  }, [url]);
  
  return { data, loading, error };
};

// Usage in component
const UserDashboard = () => {
  const { data: users, loading, error } = useDataFetching<User[]>("/api/users");
  // component logic
};
```

**5. Higher-Order Components (HOC) - Use Sparingly**
```typescript
// Use HOCs only when necessary, prefer hooks for logic sharing
const withAuth = <P extends object>(Component: React.ComponentType<P>) => {
  return (props: P) => {
    const isAuthenticated = useAppSelector(selectIsAuthenticated);
    if (!isAuthenticated) return <Navigate to="/login" />;
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
};
```

#### Composition Best Practices
- ✅ **Break down large components** into smaller, focused components
- ✅ **Use children prop** for flexible content injection
- ✅ **Create compound components** for related UI elements that work together
- ✅ **Extract custom hooks** for shared stateful logic
- ✅ **Compose at the feature level** - build features from smaller components
- ✅ **Keep components focused** - single responsibility principle
- ✅ **Use TypeScript generics** for reusable component types
- ❌ **Don't create overly nested component hierarchies** - keep it flat when possible
- ❌ **Don't pass too many props** - if >5 props, consider composition or context
- ❌ **Don't duplicate logic** - extract to hooks or utilities
- ❌ **Don't create components that do too much** - split responsibilities

#### Integration Guidelines
- When integrating components, **compose them at the parent level**
- **Pass data down, events up** - follow unidirectional data flow
- **Use context for deeply nested prop passing** - avoid prop drilling
- **Compose components in feature directories** - keep related components together
- **Export composed components** as the main API, keep sub-components internal when appropriate

## React Compiler Best Practices

### 1. Purity and Memoization
- The React Compiler automatically memoizes components and values - **DO NOT manually add useMemo/useCallback unless necessary**
- Trust the compiler to optimize - it handles memoization automatically
- Avoid premature optimization with manual memoization hooks

### 2. Compiler Directives (Use Sparingly)
- Use `"use memo"` directive only when you need to force memoization of a specific component
- Use `"use no memo"` directive only when you need to opt-out of compiler optimization
- Generally, let the compiler infer optimizations automatically

### 3. State and Props Management
- Keep state local when possible - React Compiler optimizes local state automatically
- Use Redux Toolkit (RTK) for global state management (as per project setup)
- Prefer derived state over redundant state - let compiler optimize computations

### 4. Event Handlers and Callbacks
- Define event handlers inline or as stable references
- React Compiler automatically memoizes callbacks - avoid manual useCallback unless you have a specific reason
- For callbacks passed to memoized children, the compiler handles optimization

## TypeScript Best Practices

### 1. Type Definitions
- Always define TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Use `React.FC<PropsType>` sparingly - prefer explicit return types or inference
- Import types from model interfaces when available (e.g., `model/*.interface.ts`)
- Use type imports: `import type { ... }` for type-only imports

### 2. Component Props
```typescript
// Good: Explicit interface
interface ComponentProps {
  title: string;
  count?: number;
  onAction: (id: string) => void;
}

const Component = ({ title, count = 0, onAction }: ComponentProps) => {
  // component code
};
```

### 3. Hooks Typing
- Type useState with explicit generic when type can't be inferred: `useState<string>("")`
- Type useRef appropriately: `useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null)`
- Type custom hooks with proper return types

## Project-Specific Patterns

### 1. Redux Toolkit Integration
- Use `useAppSelector` and `useAppDispatch` from `store/store.ts`
- Dispatch actions from slice files (e.g., `store/systemSlice.ts`)
- Keep Redux logic in slice files, not in components

### 2. Routing
- Use `react-router-dom` v7 patterns
- Use `useNavigate` and `useLocation` hooks
- Implement protected routes using `ProtectedRoute` component

### 3. Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS classes for styling
- Follow existing component patterns for consistency
- Use responsive design utilities (sm:, md:, lg:, etc.)

### 4. File Organization
- Components in `src/Components/` directory
- Group related components in subdirectories
- Utilities in `src/utils/` directory
- Types/interfaces in `model/` directory
- Store slices in `store/` directory

### 5. Error Handling
- Use toast notifications via `utils/toast.ts` (Error, Success functions)
- Handle async errors with try-catch in useEffect or event handlers
- Provide user-friendly error messages

### 6. Loading States
- Always handle loading states for async operations
- Use skeleton loaders (e.g., `LineChartSkeleton`) for better UX
- Set loading state before async operations, clear after completion/error

## Code Quality Standards

### 1. Imports
- Group imports: React imports, third-party, local components, utilities, types
- Use absolute imports when configured, otherwise relative imports
- Remove unused imports

### 2. Component Structure
```typescript
// 1. Imports
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";
import type { ComponentProps } from "../types";

// 2. Type definitions
interface ComponentProps {
  // props
}

// 3. Component
const Component = ({ prop1, prop2 }: ComponentProps) => {
  // 4. Hooks (in order: state, refs, redux, effects, callbacks, computed)
  const [state, setState] = useState();
  const ref = useRef();
  const dispatch = useAppDispatch();
  const data = useAppSelector(selector);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    // effects
  }, [dependencies]);
  
  const handleAction = () => {
    // handlers
  };
  
  // 5. Computed values (useMemo only if compiler can't optimize)
  const computed = expensiveCalculation();
  
  // 6. Early returns
  if (loading) return <Loader />;
  if (error) return <Error />;
  
  // 7. Render
  return (
    <div>
      {/* JSX */}
    </div>
  );
};

// 8. Export
export default Component;
```

### 3. Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `Sidebar`, `Dashboard`)
- Props interfaces: ComponentName + "Props" (e.g., `SidebarProps`)
- Functions: camelCase (e.g., `handleClick`, `calculateTotal`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for true constants, camelCase for computed
- Files: Match component name (e.g., `Sidebar.tsx`)

### 4. Comments
- Add comments for complex logic or business rules
- Explain "why" not "what" - code should be self-documenting
- Use JSDoc comments for exported functions/components when helpful

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### 1. React Compiler Anti-Patterns
- ❌ Don't manually wrap with useMemo/useCallback "just in case"
- ❌ Don't create wrapper components solely for memoization
- ❌ Don't use React.memo() unless you have a specific performance issue
- ❌ Don't mutate props or state directly

### 2. General Anti-Patterns
- ❌ Don't call hooks conditionally or in loops
- ❌ Don't use index as key in lists (use stable IDs)
- ❌ Don't create components inside render methods
- ❌ Don't forget dependency arrays in useEffect
- ❌ Don't use any type - always provide proper types
- ❌ Don't mix async/await with .then() unnecessarily

### 3. Composition Anti-Patterns
- ❌ Don't create monolithic components - break them down
- ❌ Don't pass too many props (>5) - use composition or context instead
- ❌ Don't duplicate component logic - extract to hooks
- ❌ Don't create overly nested component hierarchies
- ❌ Don't ignore children prop when component could be more flexible
- ❌ Don't tightly couple components - keep them independent and composable

## Performance Considerations

### 1. React Compiler Optimizations
- The compiler automatically:
  - Memoizes components and values
  - Optimizes re-renders
  - Hoists constants
  - Optimizes event handlers
- Trust the compiler - only optimize manually when profiling shows issues

### 2. List Rendering
- Use stable keys from data (IDs, not indices)
- Consider virtualization for long lists
- Extract list items to separate components when complex

### 3. Bundle Size
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting when appropriate
- Import only what you need from libraries
- Consider lazy loading for routes

## Testing Considerations

- Write testable components (pure functions, clear props)
- Keep business logic separate from components when possible
- Use proper TypeScript types to catch errors at compile time

## When Creating New Components

1. ✅ Check if similar component exists - reuse or extend
2. ✅ Consider composition - can this be built from existing components?
3. ✅ Define TypeScript interface for props
4. ✅ Use functional component with hooks
5. ✅ Follow the component structure pattern above
6. ✅ Use children prop for flexible composition when appropriate
7. ✅ Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
8. ✅ Handle loading and error states
9. ✅ Use project utilities (toast, error handlers)
10. ✅ Follow existing styling patterns
11. ✅ Export component properly
12. ✅ Remove unused imports and code
13. ✅ Let React Compiler handle optimizations

## When Modifying Existing Components

1. ✅ Maintain existing patterns and structure
2. ✅ Don't break existing functionality
3. ✅ Consider refactoring to composition if component is growing too large
4. ✅ Update types if props change
5. ✅ Ensure hooks are still at top level
6. ✅ Check if changes affect memoization needs
7. ✅ Update related components if interface changes
8. ✅ Test affected functionality
9. ✅ Keep component pure - move side effects to appropriate hooks
10. ✅ Extract reusable parts into separate components or hooks when appropriate

